<!DOCTYPE html><script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/texme@1.2.2"></script>
<textarea>


This document outlines how the __mmdh__ package can be used to
download and preprocess HCES 2023-24 data from the MoSPI microdata
website.

We assume that the `MOSPI_API_KEY` environment variable has been set
prior to running the code in this document.

```{r}
library(mmdh)
## confirm ID no
dlist <- list_datasets()
subset(dlist, grepl("hces", idno, ignore.case = TRUE))
```

The files available for `idno = "DDI-IND-MOSPI-NSS-HCES23-24"` can be
listed using

```{r}
hces202324_files <- list_files("DDI-IND-MOSPI-NSS-HCES23-24")
hces202324_files
```

As we can see, the data are available in several formats. We will use
the SPSS format files. We specify a destination folder to download the
corresponding ZIP file if it does not already exist.

```{r}
DESTDIR <- "HCES23-24"
ZIPFILE <- file.path(DESTDIR, "HCES_Data_2023-24_SPSS.zip")
if (!dir.exists(DESTDIR)) dir.create(DESTDIR)
if (!file.exists(ZIPFILE))
    download_file(id = "DDI-IND-MOSPI-NSS-HCES23-24",
                  base64 = "SENFU19EYXRhXzIwMjMtMjRfU1BTUy56aXA",
                  destfile = ZIPFILE)
list.files(DESTDIR)
```
 
Next we unzip the downloaded file. This is likely to take some time,
as the files are quite large.

```{r}
unzip(ZIPFILE, exdir = DESTDIR)
list.files(DESTDIR)
(savfiles <- list.files(file.path(DESTDIR, "HCES_Data_2023-24_SPSS")))
```

Our next task is to read these files, clean them up, remove repeated
columns, and save them in the R binary format. First, we rename these
files.

```{r}
savfiles2 <- substring(savfiles, 1, 10) |> paste0(".sav")
savfiles2 <- gsub(" - ", "", savfiles2, fixed = TRUE)
savfiles2
file.rename(file.path(DESTDIR, "HCES_Data_2023-24_SPSS", savfiles),
            file.path(DESTDIR, "HCES_Data_2023-24_SPSS", savfiles2)) |> stopifnot()
list.files(file.path(DESTDIR, "HCES_Data_2023-24_SPSS"))
```

Finally, we read these SAV files into R. In addition to the
observations, each SAV file repeats several columns related to survey
design. The names of these columns are stored in the __mmdh__ package:

```{r}
HCES_2324_survey_vars
```

We will remove these (redundant) columns during conversion, instead
adding a unique household identifier. Of course, we will also need the
survey variables for analysis, so we store them separately as "LEVEL
0" data.

```{r}
SAVDIR <- file.path(DESTDIR, "HCES_Data_2023-24_SPSS")
dir.create(RDSDIR <- file.path(DESTDIR, "HCES_Data_2023-24_RDS"))
level00 <- read_hces_sav(file.path(SAVDIR, "LEVEL01.sav"),
                         keep = HCES_2324_survey_vars)
names(level00)
saveRDS(level00, file = file.path(RDSDIR, "level00.rds"))
```

Finally, we convert all the SPSS data files and save in the R binary
format.

```{r}
sav2rds <- function(filename) {
    message("Processing ", filename)
    d <- read_hces_sav(file.path(SAVDIR, filename),
                       drop = HCES_2324_survey_vars)
    out <- gsub(".sav", ".rds", filename, fixed = TRUE)
    saveRDS(d, file = file.path(RDSDIR, out))
}
sapply(savfiles2, sav2rds) |> invisible()
```

The converted files should not be available.

```{r}
list.files(RDSDIR)
```


</textarea>
